            0.087,
            0.776,
            0.191
          ],
          "content": "Thus, in magnesium hydroxide, there are two hydroxide ions (OH–) for each magnesium ion $( \\mathrm { M g ^ { 2 + } } )$ . We use brackets ( ) when we have two or more polyatomic ions of the same type in a formula. In the example of aluminium hydroxide given below, the bracket around OH with a subscript 3 indicates that there are three hydroxide (OH–) ions bound to one aluminium ion. Brackets are not required when only one polyatomic anion is present."
        },
