          ],
          "content": "(i) $\\mathrm { F e ^ { 3 + } }$ and OH‒ (ii) K+ and $\\mathrm { C O _ { 3 } ^ { 2 - } }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.689,
            0.773,
            0.735
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "9.6 Properties of the Ionic and the Covalent Compounds"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.74,
            0.431,
            0.759
          ],
          "content": "**Activity 9.4: Let us experiment**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.761,
            0.773,
            0.797
          ],
          "content": "1. Collect the samples of some compounds, such as camphor, sodium chloride, copper sulfate, sugar and naphthalene."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.799,
            0.661,
            0.817
          ],
          "content": "**(A) Solubility in (i) water, (ii) kerosene, and (iii) petrol**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.82,
            0.773,
            0.839
          ],
          "content": "2. Try dissolving each sample separately in the water, kerosene and petrol."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.841,
            0.496,
            0.859
          ],
          "content": "3. Record your observations in Table 9.2."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.141,
            0.861,
            0.515,
            0.879
          ],
          "content": "**(B) Electrical conductivity in the water**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.881,
            0.774,
            0.93
          ],
          "content": "**Safety first**: Do not touch the electrodes when they are connected to the battery but use a low-voltage battery to avoid the risk of shock. Petrol and kerosene are flammable liquids, so be careful while working with them."
        }
      ]
